13
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,000
Hey Yalta, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

14
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:31,520
Hi, yeah, thanks for having me.

15
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,360
It's been a while, but I'm glad to be on it.

16
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:38,720
So I don't often dig into the kind of origin story of apps here on the podcast.

17
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:47,040
I try and jump right to the tactical, but I think this is a really interesting origin story that I think folks can learn from and it's fun and inspiring.

18
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:48,000
So let's dive in.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:48,480
Like, why?

20
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:50,480
did you build Savvy Navy?

21
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:51,600
Sure.

22
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:52,480
Yeah.

23
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:55,840
So a few years ago, I found myself in the middle of the Pacific.

24
00:01:56,960 --> 00:01:57,920
The wind was howling.

25
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:08,960
The boat was healed over at thirty grade angles and slamming through three meter high waves because I had taken a sabbatical from Google at the time to take part in the Clipper Round the World yacht race.

26
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:13,760
I had a particularly bad day in the office when I signed up to this race.

27
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:16,560
I thought I'd seen the posters many times.

28
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:18,880
I was like, oh, I want to do this at some point.

29
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,000
And then a bad day.

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,640
And I was like, okay, I'm going to sign up for this race.

31
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:24,480
I didn't even talk to my wife at the time.

32
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:26,240
So I just signed up and did it.

33
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:46,960
To be fair, there's a lot of training involved before you actually go do it, but I signed up, did it, got out on the water and just realized, I mean, this is a 70 foot multimillion pound racing yacht and it's, you know, to the brim with the latest technology, but it just it was just outdated, like a lot of the systems on this boat were like not talking to each other.

34
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,160
A lot of stuff was showing me pretty pictures, but then I'm the one who has to do all the computing in my head.

35
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:56,000
And so being Dutch and being arrogant, I thought I could do better.

36
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:58,160
So I left Google and started Cyber Navy.

37
00:02:58,720 --> 00:02:58,960
Nice.

38
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:00,960
And so back in 2017.

39
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:13,520
I mean, back before Savvy Navy started making these things easier, and this is only, I mean, we're only talking like 11 years ago, you were you were still having to like plot things on paper while you're cutting through those three meter waves, right?

40
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,000
Yeah, for a lot of it.

41
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:22,880
I mean, there were tools and in the professional space, you had different set of tools, but they were all just really clunky.

42
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:27,600
And most would focus on one particular aspect of a passage, as it's called.

43
00:03:28,240 --> 00:03:42,080
And this particular race, in case it was a race, but if you're going from A to B over water, there's a hazards, it's shipping lanes, it's dangerous areas, and it's the weather.

44
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:43,360
Like what, what is the weather doing?

45
00:03:43,520 --> 00:03:44,320
What is the wind doing?

46
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:45,600
What is the tide doing?

47
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:45,920
Right?

48
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:50,160
The body of water that you find yourself in, that itself is moving, right?

49
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:59,760
And so you have to compute this all and you have to like take all these inputs to get you safely and as fast as possible from A to B and as fast as possible in the case of a race.

50
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:06,320
And so there are tools and there were tools, but they each were focused on one particular aspect.

51
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:10,400
And so you'll find yourself using one tool going, okay, I'm going over there.

52
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:15,200
I'll be there probably in six hours fromurs from now because you're like computing how fast you're going to go with the wind.

53
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:19,440
So now I have to go over here and figure out, well, in six hours from now, what is the tide going to do?

54
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:20,320
What is the current doing?

55
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:21,200
Is it going to push me back?

56
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:21,920
Is it not?

57
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:24,240
Is it going to push me into a dangerous area?

58
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:36,320
And so, yeah, on the boat that we had, we were not really using pen and paper, but it was still effectively the same thing because I'm the one doing that work, whereas these computers could far more easily do it.

59
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:42,880
I mean, even the compute power on this guy is more powerful than, well, hey, what's in my brain, let's talk what was on that boat.

60
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:47,680
So, yeah, it just struck me as odd that you couldn't do this in a easier way, right?

61
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:51,840
At the end of the day with Google Maps, you drop two pins and it works out everything, right?

62
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:58,400
It works out the traffic and the road works and the most economically friendly way to do it.

63
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:00,160
And at sea, it should do the same thing.

64
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:06,960
And if actually in many cases, there's more reason to do it at sea because there's more danger element involved.

65
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:13,280
And so for me to compute that in my head, human error, like you, you don't want human error when it comes to lives at sea, right?

66
00:05:13,600 --> 00:05:16,800
So yeah, there was there was definitely a better way to do it.

67
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,160
So you get back from this race, you quit Google, you raise VC and you build Tavernabby, right?

68
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,520
Yeah, yeah, almost, not quite.

69
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,840
It wasn't quite that straight for it.

70
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:31,680
Actually, to be honest, I I had the idea on the race.

71
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:37,040
I wasn't initially quite convinced if, if there was really a market for this.

72
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:42,800
I mean, a 70 foot racing yacht in the middle of the Pacific, how many people are going to, you know, how many people are in that position, right?

73
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:51,280
So it wasn't until I got back and started talking to friends and other boaters and started to realize that actually, this isn't just an ocean race problem.

74
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:53,040
This is anyone going out on the water.

75
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:56,160
And here in the UK on the south coast, it's beautiful.

76
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:58,560
You know, the Soland is amazing to sail in.

77
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:45,200
And there's people that have their qualification right they've done all the training that they need to do but they weren't going out because they were afraid that they would get the title calculations wrong or that you know they would injure someone or whatever um and so it it became clear that this is a bigger problem um so i did a lot of did a lot of research um i went down to the south coast and i even bought a clipboard because not that i needed a clipboard but anybody with a clipboard is taken seriously right so if you just rock up and ask a question nobody answers but well there's a clipboard perhaps people will answer so i spent hours literally hours walking up and down every pontoon i could find to talk to any boater i could find to just understand how they go boating and, you know, not just sailors but motorboaters, even kayakers and paddleboarders and anyone else getting on the water.

78
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:46,640
What's the pain point?

79
00:06:46,880 --> 00:06:50,160
What is the real issue with you enjoying and getting on the water more?

80
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:56,720
Yeah, too too many people skip that stuff and and you don't it doesn't have to be a clipboard.

81
00:06:56,880 --> 00:07:01,680
It doesn't have to be in person, but the user research and like deeply understanding the problem.

82
00:07:02,320 --> 00:07:05,520
And it sounds like that's what really convinced you that this was a bigger market.

83
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:08,240
There are markets where people are like, ah, it's not that big.

84
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:10,880
Like you were thinking like I was on a racing yacht.

85
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:14,800
There's only a handful of those in the world that only happens certain times of the year.

86
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:16,400
Like, how big is this market really?

87
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:22,400
And then by going and doing the research and understanding the pain points, like, oh wait, like the boating market is actually huge.

88
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:24,720
And like everyone's solving, seeing this problem.

89
00:07:25,760 --> 00:07:29,520
So that user research piece is, is, is, is way too often skipped.

90
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:31,280
And so it's really cool that you did that.

91
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:33,840
It's often skipped and that's, that's bad.

92
00:07:34,240 --> 00:07:35,920
But, um, it doesn't stop there either.

93
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:36,240
Right?

94
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:39,600
Like for me, it was like, okay, this clearly there is something here.

95
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:40,720
And so I can build this.

96
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:42,720
But to this day, we do this.

97
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:43,040
Right?

98
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:46,880
To this day, we talk to our customers for new features, whatever.

99
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:49,200
And to this day, we learn new things., right?

100
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:59,360
If we look at what we did originally and then what we're looking at now and even geographically, like obviously a boater in Italy is going to have different pain points than somebody in the UK, right?

101
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:01,680
There's no tides in Italy to start with, right?

102
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:08,880
So 100% people forget that step and or they do it for a little bit and then they just, that's it, we're going to write on this info.

103
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:10,400
You got to keep doing it.

104
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:15,760
You got to keep talking to the customer and understand even over time, how does it change?

105
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:19,520
People will use tools differently over the last five years, let alone anything else.

106
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,360
So yeah, keep talking for sure.

107
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,640
One of the things I love about this story is that there really are.

108
00:08:25,280 --> 00:08:31,120
parallels to today in bringing AI into the equation.

109
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:44,560
So, you know, a decade ago, you were at Google, you were using Google Maps in your car, you probably had CarPlay and other things even a decade ago that just didn't exist in the boating world.

110
00:08:45,280 --> 00:08:52,000
And then today, I think folks listening to this can be thinking whether they have an existing product or whether they want to build a new product.

111
00:08:52,640 --> 00:09:00,480
So much of what we think is like great tech today is going to feel super clumsy over the next few years as you can integrate AI.

112
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,000
deeper and deeper into it.

113
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:05,840
I just saw a tweet yesterday from someone participating in the Shipaton.

114
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:08,960
It's a hackathon we're doing starting August 1st.

115
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:10,960
So this, this podcast will come out right after it.

116
00:09:11,520 --> 00:09:16,960
Um, but he's building a cataloging app for all your games and things like that.

117
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:29,840
And he built this AI feature that he was showing off on Twitter yesterday where you just like take your phone and like just like scan it around and it grabs all the titles and then like brings it in and makes it all look nice.

118
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:49,200
So instead of like typing in every game thing, it's just using, there's so much opportunity to kind of bring AI, bring tech, bring this product intuition into these, you know, clunky industries.

119
00:09:50,400 --> 00:10:04,560
So tell me a little bit about how, how you thought about that in building Savi Navi coming from Google and coming into this really clunky old industry with all these like outdated tools and low processing power and all the things you were facing at the time.

120
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:05,520
Yeah, for sure.

121
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:06,560
And I totally agree.

122
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:10,160
I think, you know, when I started with Savi Navi, I was at Google Maps.

123
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:52,640
And so I had a lot of experience not just google maps i did google chrome did a bunch of different things at google but a lot of experience in that user interface and that that um that interaction model that becomes so that we're all so accustomed to right now which wasn't and to a degree isn't still in marine nowadays if you've got a product any tech product that if it comes with a manual you've already lost like fundamentally people don't use a manual i just use the product and it should just be obvious on how i should use it and that's not something that was happening in marine i think in marine everything is about if i'm nice let's say 10 If I'm nice, I'd say ten years behind land based stuff, probably fifteen to twenty years, quite frankly.

124
00:10:53,120 --> 00:11:01,600
And so I took a lot of that learning that I had from Google into SaviNavi and made it a tool that is just immediately recognizable.

125
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:04,000
And I know exactly what I'm doing and how I'm using it.

126
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:05,760
And I think you're absolutely right with AI.

127
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:18,000
If we look back at before ChatGP came out and had said, oh, you know, in a few months from now, you're going to be talking to, you know, your computer or whatever else or typing and just doing natural language, everybody would have gotten away.

128
00:11:18,240 --> 00:11:18,400
Right?

129
00:11:19,040 --> 00:11:21,520
And then within months, everybody's doing it.

130
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:25,840
And not only is everybody doing it, but if ChatGPT gets something wrong something wrong now, we get annoyed.

131
00:11:25,920 --> 00:11:27,440
Like, come on, it should know better, right?

132
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:32,720
Like, we're already so accustomed to talking to an AI and to get a response.

133
00:11:33,440 --> 00:11:35,760
And so that's how quickly that's changed, right?

134
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:46,960
And so if you're looking at products and you're looking at stuff on your phone or whatever, that's going to, it already is, I think, rapidly changing, but it's going to increasingly more rapidly change, if that makes sense.

135
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:49,600
And so the expectation, again, is different.

136
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:51,280
And I think that's the key thing here.

137
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:54,720
You have to put yourself in the consumer's shoes, right?

138
00:11:55,120 --> 00:11:59,040
And from a consumer perspective, I expect this thing to work in a certain way.

139
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:00,480
And that expectation is.

140
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:01,680
constantly changing.

141
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:11,920
And again, this is why you need to keep talking to your customer because in twelve months from now, I guarantee they will expect something completely different because of AI and because of how you interact with said product.

142
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:16,960
So yeah, it's a never ending thing and you have to stay on top of it.

143
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:21,440
And if you're building anything with the intent of launching it in twelve months from now, you've already lost, right?

144
00:12:21,520 --> 00:12:23,520
Because in twelve months from now, that's going to be outdated.

145
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:26,400
You need to be able to move quick enough to be able to do that.

146
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:30,000
I love that idea of expectations have changed.

147
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:36,560
And that's maybe like a really good rubric for thinking about products to build or ways to improve your own product.

148
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:59,760
is that if you find yourself frustrated, you know, even my example of the guy building the game scanning app to build a library of all your games, you know, in 2026, the idea of pulling up your iPhone and typing things in is crazy, like the expectation is like, oh, AI, like everything's so smart, like why am I doing this?

149
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:12,240
And so it's probably a good way to think about opportunities is like, what are the things that are frustrating today in 2026 that seem really cool like three years ago or whatever, but in 2026.

150
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:13,760
It's like, this is dumb.

151
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:14,960
Like, why am I doing this?

152
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:20,000
And it's a great way to think about improving your product and even like finding new product ideas.

153
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:20,640
Yeah.

154
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:28,640
And, and, you know, coming back to Savannah, as I said, when I started, it was like this concept of it should be as simple as dropping a pin and say, go, right?

155
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:30,640
Like, I want to go here, so drop a pin and go.

156
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:36,560
The concept of dropping a pin, you could argue by now is starting to become slightly dated as well.

157
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:38,960
Like, why am I dropping a pin over here?

158
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:41,840
I want to go to Cows, which is a city on the island, right?

159
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:45,280
I just want to go, hey, Savannah, tell me when I should go to Cows.

160
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:46,240
What's the best time to go?

161
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:47,680
Well, Saturday, 11 o'clock.

162
00:13:47,840 --> 00:13:48,640
Okay, great, right?

163
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:49,840
And then show me that route, right?

164
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:54,720
So even things like that, I think, are so quickly changing and turning.

165
00:13:56,160 --> 00:14:00,240
And the beauty of software is that you can, you can adapt quickly.

166
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,640
You don't have long lead times when it comes to hardware.

167
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:09,520
We are doing a lot more stuff with boat manufacturers now, which has opened my eyes to that world of supply chain and whatever.

168
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:11,840
But you have to, you have to move fast.

169
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,720
You have to be able to move fast because it's what people will expect and already are.

170
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:19,040
I want to talk more about the boat manufacturers.

171
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:24,320
You're doing some really cool stuff with B to B that creates a flywheel with B to C.

172
00:14:25,040 --> 00:14:26,720
But I did want to kind of continue the story.

173
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:35,120
I joked earlier about, so you quit Google and raised VC, because I knew that wasn't the way, but I want to talk about the way, because you did think about raising VC.

174
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:36,240
So what, what happened there?

175
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:51,600
Yeah, if you like, I left Google and if you'd have asked me at that time, do you understand all the various different funding vehicles out there, VC, private equity, debt, crowdfunding, whatever, I would have said yes, because, you know, they're all the same, right?

176
00:14:51,920 --> 00:14:55,680
You get investors, they give you money, they get a share of the company and off you go.

177
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:58,160
And an angel is just a small VC, right?

178
00:14:58,560 --> 00:14:59,360
That's how that works, right?

179
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:02,160
And it's so not true, like it's so different.

180
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:11,520
Um, you know, I left and luckily because David and Google was invited to a Google pitch event and a pitch day at the time, we didn't actually need funding yet.

181
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:14,400
Um, I was, you know, me and my co-founder were bootstrapping.

182
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,600
Um, but you get invited by Google, you know, you're going to go, right?

183
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:35,840
And so we did a pitch and had some interest from a number of VCs, sat down with one of them, multiple times, flew out to them, whatever, and went very far down that standard path of getting funding from VC only to realize, as we're talking, they want one thing from the business, we want something else.

184
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:42,720
And actually, even as we're discussing the term sheet, it's already a little bit, there's already friction, right?

185
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:44,880
There's already something here that doesn't feel quite right.

186
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:50,160
And so ultimately, to be fair from both sides, we walked away and we said, okay, well, this doesn't feel right.

187
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:51,760
This is not what we want from the business.

188
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,480
We're going to, and we didn't need it, so we walked away.

189
00:15:55,280 --> 00:16:04,800
And then about a year later, one of our potential angels and a very good friend and customer actually said, have you, have you considered crowdfunding?

190
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:08,480
Because at the time, we had 10,000 people on the platform using the app, et cetera.

191
00:16:09,520 --> 00:16:10,400
And so we hadn't.

192
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:12,000
