00:17:53,600 --> 00:18:00,720
We got 10,000 people using the app and sure, I have a network of people that potentially have some, you know, spare capital, but I don't know.

216
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:02,640
And we only needed a little bit of funding.

217
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:03,280
And you're right.

218
00:18:03,440 --> 00:18:05,680
We needed the funding because we wanted to push it into marketing.

219
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:09,440
At the end of the day, we're a B2C business predominantly.

220
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:17,680
And so in order for us to get to the number of subscribers that we need to get to to cover our fixed cost, you're going to need capital, right?

221
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:22,560
And so we launched this campaign, did this video for it.

222
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:25,120
No idea what to expect.

223
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:27,680
And within 24 hours, we're like oversubscribed.

224
00:18:27,760 --> 00:18:35,200
Like we're blown past the target that we needed and people are jumping on the bandwagon and mostly boaters, right?

225
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:36,720
People going, yeah, I'm done.

226
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:37,520
with what I have.

227
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:38,240
I want something new.

228
00:18:38,480 --> 00:18:39,360
I want something fresh.

229
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:45,200
And so within six days, we shut it down because it was triple of what we needed.

230
00:18:45,840 --> 00:19:00,080
And since then, have repeated that a number of times because we basically have this army of investors who A believe in what we do, B can back us with money, but also back us with knowledge.

231
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:07,120
Like we've had a number of times throughout these years that you start a business, there's so many aspects of the business you don't actually know about, right?

232
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:09,600
You don't know what you don't know until you don't know it, right?

233
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:12,400
And so at some point we needed something for logistics or something.

234
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:13,920
in marketing, whatever.

235
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:18,000
And we can tap into these 2,500 investors and go, Hey guys, we need some help with this.

236
00:19:18,320 --> 00:19:22,720
Can anyone give us some advice or jump in a call or even tell us where to go?

237
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:35,920
And so it's been really, really good for us to have that army of investors that can help us, not just financially, but also in knowledge based stuff and in marketing and in other aspects.

238
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:37,440
So yeah, it's been fascinating.

239
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:38,400
It's been really, really good.

240
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:48,480
So I'm fascinated and I think our audience will be as well because actually two podcasts ago, I actually had a venture capitalist on.

241
00:19:48,960 --> 00:20:01,760
And we were actually talking about this specifically and I hadn't even thought to bring up crowdfunding, but we were talking about how VC just doesn't make sense for so many bootstrap or so many consumer apps.

242
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:03,520
It just doesn't make sense to take VC.

243
00:20:03,920 --> 00:20:19,520
The ditch ham isn't big enough or the opportunity's great, but then there's so many, you know, sacrifices where when you're shooting for those big outcomes and you're spending a lot of money, you as a founder can end up screwed where you otherwise would have had a really amazing outcome.

244
00:20:20,160 --> 00:20:23,920
So we talked through all this and it didn't, like crowdfunding didn't even come up.

245
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:32,080
So I want to dig into like the mechanics of it because I think there's people listening to this who can like learn from this and go do some crowdfunding.

246
00:20:32,560 --> 00:20:42,000
Now, it shows an incredible level of product market fit that you have two thousand people investing in it because they believe in it.

247
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:46,000
But let's start with like just the simple mechanics of it.

248
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:51,760
Like, you know, on Kickstarter, which is what I know, you know, you give perks and things like that.

249
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:59,040
Is it similar where where like every dollar in gets a certain ownership percentage, but then you also offer certain perks and things?

250
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:00,800
So how did you actually structure that?

251
00:21:01,760 --> 00:21:02,240
It can be.

252
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:04,160
And for some of the brands we did, it was.

253
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:15,920
I think before I jump into the mechanics of that, I think just coming back to VC, I think one of the things that I learned throughout this process, look, I have nothing against the VC model as such.

254
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:23,040
I think it's a great model and it works really well, but it works really well for a very particular set of businesses, right?

255
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:29,200
And unfortunately, and I don't know, I'm presuming you've seen Silicon Valley did TV show.

256
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:30,240
I think everybody should watch it.

257
00:21:30,400 --> 00:21:31,280
It's an amazing show.

258
00:21:32,320 --> 00:21:35,200
Unfortunately, everybody believes that VC is the path, right?

259
00:21:35,360 --> 00:21:36,400
That is how you build a business.

260
00:21:37,760 --> 00:21:45,200
I think for 80%, 90%, I'm making up a number here, but for a large percentage of businesses, it's probably the wrong model, right?

261
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:46,880
It's probably not the model you want.

262
00:21:47,040 --> 00:21:51,200
You can build a really successful business that isn't necessarily hyper growth.

263
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:54,960
You're not going to be the next Mark Zuckerberg, but that doesn't mean the business is crap.

264
00:21:55,120 --> 00:21:56,480
That just means it's a different business, right?

265
00:21:56,800 --> 00:21:56,880
Yeah.

266
00:21:57,360 --> 00:22:07,200
And what's funny is like a ton of VCs would actually agree very strongly with those statements because they don't want to fund the kind of businesses that aren't a good fit for VCs.

267
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:14,880
So even VCs would 100% agree, like it is a great product, but it's a great product for for a very narrow set of people.

268
00:22:15,440 --> 00:22:27,440
Whereas, like, and exactly like you said, everyone in tech wants it's like this, it's almost like this, this weird like dream of like, oh, I'll really have made it if I can get venture capital investment.

269
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:31,680
And like, it's just not, that's not the path for so many businesses.

270
00:22:31,840 --> 00:22:35,920
Like there's so many great opportunities for great outcomes where you don't take VC.

271
00:22:36,160 --> 00:22:38,240
And if you did, it would actually mess your business up.

272
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:40,480
So I think it's so important for people to understand.

273
00:22:40,960 --> 00:22:41,520
Yeah, absolutely.

274
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:42,480
And you're right.

275
00:22:42,640 --> 00:22:45,520
Like VC will back me on this one.

276
00:22:46,240 --> 00:22:47,840
Funny phrase there to back me.

277
00:22:47,920 --> 00:23:02,320
But like they will agree., like fundamentally, in some cases there's almost like a Venn diagram overlap and that's where it gets even more quirky because then the VC goes, actually, okay, I can't if you just change this bit, then maybe you could, but now you're changing that business, right?

278
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,080
And that doesn't necessarily help.

279
00:23:04,320 --> 00:23:10,320
And so you're trying to, what's the saying, a round peg, square hole, like it doesn't necessarily fit.

280
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:16,000
Whereas if you're looking at like an angel, and again, when I started, I thought an angel is a mini VC, right?

281
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:19,680
They put money in, they want to get a return on investment just like the VC does.

282
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:20,960
But they don't.

283
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:24,560
They do, don't get me wrong, everyone wants, you know, you're investing, you want to make money.

284
00:23:25,760 --> 00:23:28,880
But a VC, when they invest, they don't just invest once, right?

285
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:30,080
They invest multiple times.

286
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:34,880
They want you to spend the money that they put in in order to grow, to get that hyper growth.

287
00:23:35,360 --> 00:23:36,400
They want you to spend all of it.

288
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:38,480
And they want you to come back for some more, right?

289
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:41,600
Pre-seed, seed, Series A, Series B, Series C.

290
00:23:42,160 --> 00:23:47,360
The reason they want that, and again, they will agree with me, is so that they own more of the business, right?

291
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:51,440
They want to own as much of this business as possible so that when there is an exit, they make the most.

292
00:23:51,600 --> 00:23:56,000
And that's how they get the 20x on the fund that they want to, you know, that they need to, need to do.

293
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:02,640
And so the object is to own as much of the business and to have that business grow at a hyper growth rate.

294
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:08,400
If you're looking at an angel, 99 out of 10 out of 100 times, the angel will have made their money.

295
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:10,160
They're retired.

296
00:24:11,280 --> 00:24:15,200
Their partner probably has kicked them out of the house going, you're doing my head in, go do something with your life.

297
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:16,800
And they want to give back, right?

298
00:24:16,960 --> 00:24:20,720
Yes, they want to invest and make money, but they want to see the business succeed.

299
00:24:21,120 --> 00:24:22,880
And that is a very different motivation.

300
00:24:23,520 --> 00:24:25,840
They don't necessarily want to own as much of the business as possible.

301
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:30,400
They want to see it succeed, not just by putting money in, but putting their expertise in.

302
00:24:30,560 --> 00:24:34,160
And they will invest in a vertical that they're familiar with because they've done it before.

303
00:24:34,800 --> 00:24:35,920
And so you get both.

304
00:24:36,400 --> 00:24:38,240
You get the best of both worlds, right?

305
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:41,920
You get someone who knows what they're talking about, who has some capital to invest.

306
00:24:42,320 --> 00:24:48,960
Now, you're not going to get the level of capital that you would get from VC, but quite frankly, in the vast majority of cases, people raise too much.

307
00:24:49,520 --> 00:24:51,120
They go, oh, I want this, I want this.

308
00:24:51,680 --> 00:25:01,840
I guarantee you, all the founders I've talked to who asked me for advice on crowdfunding, whatever, in the majority of cases, I would tell them raise half of what you're trying to raise.

309
00:25:02,320 --> 00:25:05,440
Because whatever you're trying to raise is you're, that's the other thing.

310
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:07,520
I actually hate the phrase raising money.

311
00:25:07,760 --> 00:25:10,720
Like you said, people think it's a, oh, I've made it if I raise money.

312
00:25:10,960 --> 00:25:18,560
You know, like it's not some magic money tree that you put some water in and you're raising this magical free money, you're selling your business.

313
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:20,240
You know, that is what you're doing.

314
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:23,040
And even with crowdfunding, you are selling part of your business.

315
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:27,920
So if you're doing that and you're getting a lot of money in, you're selling a lot of the business, right?

316
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:28,240
So...

317
00:25:29,360 --> 00:25:30,640
Yeah, so back to crowdfunding.

318
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:35,920
Crowdfunding, the better way to look at it when it's crowdfunding for equity is indeed angels.

319
00:25:36,240 --> 00:25:42,880
So the majority of your round will be funded by a handful of angels who put in a significant amount of money.

320
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:48,800
Now the long tail is everybody else who's putting in 100 bucks or 10 bucks or 500 bucks or a few.

321
00:25:48,800 --> 00:25:50,240
or 500 bucks or a few grand or whatever else.

322
00:25:51,280 --> 00:25:54,400
And yes, it's a platform like Kickstarter or IndyCogo.

323
00:25:54,880 --> 00:25:58,720
You go on the platform, there's a video there that explains what this business is doing.

324
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:03,200
There's some documentation and some financials and, you know, a pitch deck and whatever.

325
00:26:03,840 --> 00:26:06,080
And in some cases, in most cases, there'll be some perks.

326
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:12,400
I think in the first round, all of our investors got a Save and Avey sailing jacket, like a really cool jacket with Save and Avey in the back.

327
00:26:13,360 --> 00:26:23,280
And one, we gave people three year subscriptions because again, most of our investors are actually boaters, but the big reason you do it is to get equity into that business.

328
00:26:23,600 --> 00:26:29,280
And the ultimate aim is is always, well, it's not always a trade sale, but there is an exit in mind so that you get your money back.

329
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,960
In most of these platforms, there's then a secondary market as well.

330
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:38,880
So actually, even though we're not a public company, our shares and stocks are being traded on this secondary market.

331
00:26:39,280 --> 00:26:46,400
So even if you invested and we haven't yet made an exit, you can still liquefy some of the money by selling it on the secondary market.

332
00:26:46,800 --> 00:26:48,240
So it's a really good platform.

333
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:59,360
It's very founder friendly as well, because again, you're not dealing with a VC who's trying to mold your business into their model and trying to change what you're trying to do.

334
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:01,360
And again, that's nothing wrong.

335
00:27:01,440 --> 00:27:05,600
If you're building a business that fits the VC model, go at it any day, all day long.

336
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:07,680
But in most cases, you probably don't.

337
00:27:08,080 --> 00:27:10,560
And there's also nothing to stop people from doing both, right?

338
00:27:10,880 --> 00:27:16,800
It's very common for people to say, okay, we're going to fund via crowdfunding initially, a couple of rounds, whatever.

339
00:27:16,960 --> 00:27:20,320
And now we're going to get institutional money in to back us further.

340
00:27:21,120 --> 00:27:23,440
What was the distribution like for you?

341
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:26,400
Were there a few angels that put in 100k plus?

342
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:30,320
And actually, I'd love to know, like, what that first round looked like.

343
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:32,320
Was you raising 100,000?

344
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:34,240
Was you raising 500,000?

345
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:36,640
And then, you know, kind of what that distribution looked like?

346
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:40,880
Yeah, so the first round we our target was 125,000.

347
00:27:41,760 --> 00:27:44,240
And I think we stopped at 370 or something like that.

348
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:46,800
I'm like, okay, well, we definitely have enough now.

349
00:27:48,400 --> 00:27:54,640
And then from there on in, I'd have to remind myself on the numbers, but most of our rounds will have been around the million mark.

350
00:27:55,840 --> 00:28:02,480
And in most rounds, there would have been two or three angel investors that come in at six digits each.

351
00:28:03,040 --> 00:28:07,680
And then the rest is then the long tail of other investors.

352
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:10,000
So then what are the mechanics of it?

353
00:28:10,240 --> 00:28:12,240
Like how much ownership do you give up?

354
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:17,920
Is there like do they get profit share distributions or is it only if there's an exit?

355
00:28:18,160 --> 00:28:21,440
And then I mean, in VC you have the what's it called?

356
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:23,040
I always forget the preferential share.

357
00:28:23,440 --> 00:28:24,000
Preferred shares.

358
00:28:24,080 --> 00:28:24,480
Yeah, yeah.

359
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:27,920
So then like do they get their money back first before you get a profit?

360
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:31,280
Like walk me through the actual mechanics of that.

361
00:28:31,680 --> 00:28:36,480
So when it comes to how much equity you give up, just like any funding round, you set a valuation.

362
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:43,840
And usually if you're even if you're VCs or even you have maybe multiple VCs, there will be a lead investor, right?

363
00:28:44,160 --> 00:28:52,160
And you basically negotiate the term sheet with the lead investor, which sets the valuation and therefore sets how much equity you're willing to give up.

364
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:53,360
That's no different really.

365
00:28:53,840 --> 00:29:04,080
Fundamentally, when you're thinking about crowdfunding, before that round goes live, you will be talking to a handful select people that you have identified who might be interested.

366
00:29:04,320 --> 00:29:14,000
And so you fundamentally with them agree the terms and in large part agree what the valuation of the company is and therefore how much equity you give up.

367
00:29:15,040 --> 00:29:20,240
Once you have that agreed, then everybody else jumps on the bandwagon at the same term sheet.

368
00:29:20,720 --> 00:29:30,800
And that term sheet, again, for most platforms and definitely the one Cedars slash Republic that we do, it is very, very simple in the sense that there are no preferred shares.

369
00:29:31,120 --> 00:29:32,240
Everybody gets the same share.

370
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:41,440
Even the guy that's putting in 10 bucks versus the guy that's putting in 200,000, they all get the same level of share and nobody has a preferred share.

371
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:43,360
And so how do they get the money back?

372
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:48,960
Yes, that is generally speaking by the exit and that could be that we IPO.

373
00:29:49,200 --> 00:29:54,960
And so, okay, now they can either sell or dividends come their way or whatever or a trade sale, right?

374
00:29:55,440 --> 00:29:57,040
This is usually the most common too.

375
00:29:59,840 --> 00:30:10,000
But yeah, I think the mechanics are not that different from any other funding round in the sense that you have to agree with someone what your valuation is.

376
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:16,480
I can argue that we're worth half a billion, but if nobody is willing to put money into that valuation, then clearly we're not.

377
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:18,480
You're only worth what someone is going to put in.

378
00:30:18,800 --> 00:30:20,240
And so it's the same concept here.

379
00:30:20,560 --> 00:30:24,960
You will have angels with whom you have that connection and you sit down with them and work that out.

380
00:30:25,440 --> 00:30:27,280
And then everyone else comes on the same terms.

381
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:32,560
I would imagine because you're doing this publicly that the valuations are public.

